llvm: do not require dlopen to build an execution engine

Why: EngineBuilder::create() loads the running program as a dynamic library so
the JIT can resolve symbols out of it. A fully static build has no dlopen, that
call reports failure, and create() returns nullptr -- leaving llvmpipe with no
JIT at all.

What: compile the call out unless the build reports dlopen support. A static
executable has no dynamic symbol table for that lookup to search in the first
place. The packaged llvmpipe render/readback test is what covers the JIT this
produces.

diff --git a/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp b/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
--- a/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
+++ b/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
@@ -508,8 +508,10 @@ ExecutionEngine *EngineBuilder::create(TargetMachine *TM) {
 
   // Make sure we can resolve symbols in the program as well. The zero arg
   // to the function tells DynamicLibrary to load the program, not a library.
+#if defined(HAVE_DLOPEN)
   if (sys::DynamicLibrary::LoadLibraryPermanently(nullptr, ErrorStr))
     return nullptr;
+#endif
 
   // If the user specified a memory manager but didn't specify which engine to
   // create, we assume they only want the JIT, and we fail if they only want
